00:02:26.165 --> 00:02:28.170
Podcast" if I didn't
talk about Kubernetes.

00:02:28.170 --> 00:02:30.600
Many of you probably
noticed late last year

00:02:30.600 --> 00:02:33.660
1.9 was announced as available.

00:02:33.660 --> 00:02:36.734
It hasn't hit GKE yet,
but the blog series

00:02:36.734 --> 00:02:38.400
that they do on a
regular basis whenever

00:02:38.400 --> 00:02:41.310
they do a new release called
the Five Days of Kubernetes

00:02:41.310 --> 00:02:42.990
has started for 1.9.

00:02:42.990 --> 00:02:44.930
They've done the first,
I want to say four--

00:02:44.930 --> 00:02:46.260
one, two, three, four.

00:02:46.260 --> 00:02:47.850
They talk about
Windows support which

00:02:47.850 --> 00:02:50.280
has gone beta in Kubernetes,
which is really cool;

00:02:50.280 --> 00:02:52.490
storage; the CSI framework
which is in alpha;

00:02:52.490 --> 00:02:54.616
a bunch of stuff around
dynamic admission controls;

00:02:54.616 --> 00:02:56.490
client-go, so basically
the stuff if you want

00:02:56.490 --> 00:02:58.790
to do custom controllers;
and your own sort of custom

00:02:58.790 --> 00:03:01.320
resource definitions,
basically extend Kubernetes.

00:03:01.320 --> 00:03:03.600
So we'll probably find,
at some point, some people

00:03:03.600 --> 00:03:05.760
to come in and talk to us
about this kind of stuff.

00:03:05.760 --> 00:03:07.634
If anyone on the Kubernetes
team is listening

00:03:07.634 --> 00:03:09.810
and want to come join us,
we would love to have you.

00:03:09.810 --> 00:03:11.524
But if not, definitely
go check out

00:03:11.524 --> 00:03:12.690
the Five Days of Kubernetes.

00:03:12.690 --> 00:03:14.000
They're always a good read.

00:03:14.000 --> 00:03:15.750
MELANIE WARRICK: And
we've also got listed

00:03:15.750 --> 00:03:17.234
in there the Kubernetes comic--

00:03:17.234 --> 00:03:18.900
MARK MANDEL: Oh my
God, that is so good.

00:03:18.900 --> 00:03:20.280
MELANIE WARRICK: --that
is out there that's

00:03:20.280 --> 00:03:21.640
Smooth Sailing with Kubernetes.

00:03:21.640 --> 00:03:23.431
It's a fun little comic
that helps give you

00:03:23.431 --> 00:03:25.050
an understanding of this space.

00:03:25.050 --> 00:03:26.040
MARK MANDEL: Yeah,
no, I love this.

00:03:26.040 --> 00:03:27.498
If you've ever
thought to yourself,

00:03:27.498 --> 00:03:30.030
I just don't quite get where
