00:03:30.030 --> 00:03:33.060
as a whole, how that works,
or why people use them

00:03:33.060 --> 00:03:35.280
or what's the impetus
behind that kind of stuff,

00:03:35.280 --> 00:03:37.770
it's a really great,
light-hearted way of looking

00:03:37.770 --> 00:03:40.170
at the ecosystem and looking
at why these things exist

00:03:40.170 --> 00:03:42.210
and why people use them
and how they use them.

00:03:42.210 --> 00:03:43.360
And it's just adorable.

00:03:43.360 --> 00:03:44.040
MELANIE WARRICK: It is adorable.

00:03:44.040 --> 00:03:46.110
And it's fun to see all
the different metaphors

00:03:46.110 --> 00:03:47.160
that they're using throughout.

00:03:47.160 --> 00:03:49.410
All right, well, I think
it's time to go talk to Sam.

00:03:49.410 --> 00:03:50.910
MARK MANDEL: Let's go do that.

00:03:50.910 --> 00:03:54.450
So today I am both
excited and delighted

00:03:54.450 --> 00:03:58.250
to have Sam Ramji here with
us, VP of Product Management

00:03:58.250 --> 00:04:00.419
at Google Cloud Platform.

00:04:00.419 --> 00:04:01.710
So very happy to have you here.

00:04:01.710 --> 00:04:02.510
How are you doing today, Sam?

00:04:02.510 --> 00:04:03.450
SAM RAMJI: I'm doing fantastic.

00:04:03.450 --> 00:04:04.770
Thank you, Mark, and
thank you, Melanie.

00:04:04.770 --> 00:04:05.130
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:04:05.130 --> 00:04:06.720
Thank you for the
wine that you brought.

00:04:06.720 --> 00:04:07.595
That was very lovely.

00:04:07.595 --> 00:04:10.134
SAM RAMJI: It seemed
like only good manners.

00:04:10.134 --> 00:04:11.550
MARK MANDEL: Yeah,
we totally want

00:04:11.550 --> 00:04:14.460
to talk to you today about sort
of your thoughts about tech

00:04:14.460 --> 00:04:15.929
and cloud and open source.

00:04:15.929 --> 00:04:17.970
But for those people who
don't know who you are--

00:04:17.970 --> 00:04:19.410
I'm sure there aren't
very many of those--

00:04:19.410 --> 00:04:21.029
do you want to tell
us a little about you

00:04:21.029 --> 00:04:23.430
and your history here at Google
Cloud and what do you do?

00:04:23.430 --> 00:04:24.263
SAM RAMJI: Oh, sure.

00:04:24.263 --> 00:04:26.730
So I've been here for about
a total of 13 and 1/2 months.

00:04:26.730 --> 00:04:28.230
True fact, I just
met somebody today

00:04:28.230 --> 00:04:29.880
who's been here
13 and 1/2 years,

00:04:29.880 --> 00:04:31.530
exactly 12 times longer than me.

00:04:31.530 --> 00:04:32.430
MELANIE WARRICK: Wow.

00:04:32.430 --> 00:04:34.110
SAM RAMJI: And so I
have much to learn,

00:04:34.110 --> 00:04:35.640
although I've
learned as much as I

00:04:35.640 --> 00:04:37.652
could in the intervening time.

00:04:37.652 --> 00:04:39.360
I've been in the
industry about 23 years.

00:04:39.360 --> 00:04:40.740
As my family will
tell you, I'm not

00:04:40.740 --> 00:04:42.281
good at really
anything but software.

00:04:42.281 --> 00:04:45.570
I started programming when
I was nine in 1980 when

00:04:45.570 --> 00:04:48.100
they put in the
Commodore PET 2001Ns

00:04:48.100 --> 00:04:50.312
into the Oakland Unified
Public School District.

00:04:50.312 --> 00:04:52.020
Never thought of
programming as a career,

00:04:52.020 --> 00:04:54.395
although I kind of accidentally
was a teacher's assistant

00:04:54.395 --> 00:04:56.310
for AP Computer Science.

00:04:56.310 --> 00:04:59.140
I'm old enough that that
was Pascal at the time.

00:04:59.140 --> 00:05:00.390
MELANIE WARRICK: I did Pascal.

00:05:00.390 --> 00:05:01.245
MARK MANDEL: I
did not do Pascal.

00:05:01.245 --> 00:05:01.920
MELANIE WARRICK: I
know about Pascal.

00:05:01.920 --> 00:05:03.503
SAM RAMJI: Yeah, so
that's kind of me.

00:05:03.503 --> 00:05:07.480
I'm fascinated by distributed
systems, distributed cognition.

00:05:07.480 --> 00:05:10.790
I have a CS degree but
it's not computer science.

00:05:10.790 --> 00:05:14.710
It's cognitive science
from UC San Diego--

00:05:14.710 --> 00:05:17.870
also the home of Pascal,
strange coincidence.

00:05:17.870 --> 00:05:20.590
But it's artificial
intelligence, neuroscience,

00:05:20.590 --> 00:05:22.900
and then also things like
cognitive psychology,

00:05:22.900 --> 00:05:24.070
linguistics.

00:05:24.070 --> 00:05:26.086
I'm just fascinated
by how people

00:05:26.086 --> 00:05:27.460
think, how we
think at scale, how

00:05:27.460 --> 00:05:29.500
we think with each
other, both the success

00:05:29.500 --> 00:05:30.720
cases and the failure cases.

00:05:30.720 --> 00:05:32.720
And that I think is so
much of what lights me up

00:05:32.720 --> 00:05:36.610
about cloud computing, about
large-scale digital enablement.

00:05:36.610 --> 00:05:39.460
It's about being better and
more interesting humans together

00:05:39.460 --> 00:05:41.470
and starting to ask
the questions, well,

00:05:41.470 --> 00:05:44.050
what's possible now that
used to be impossible,

00:05:44.050 --> 00:05:45.520
and how much father can we go?

00:05:45.520 --> 00:05:46.470
MELANIE WARRICK:
Well, and if you've

00:05:46.470 --> 00:05:48.261
got this background
with cognitive science,

00:05:48.261 --> 00:05:50.860
what do you think about what's
going on in the AI revolution

00:05:50.860 --> 00:05:52.287
that we're having right now?

00:05:52.287 --> 00:05:53.620
SAM RAMJI: Oh, it's fascinating.

00:05:53.620 --> 00:05:55.869
I mean, I don't know what
the reverse of schadenfreude

00:05:55.869 --> 00:05:59.660
is, but I graduated in 1994
into the second AI winter,

00:05:59.660 --> 00:06:02.577
right when the promise of
AI had been way overpromised

00:06:02.577 --> 00:06:03.410
and then it crashed.

00:06:03.410 --> 00:06:05.180
And nobody wanted to hire us.

00:06:05.180 --> 00:06:07.690
It was like kind of go to
the back of the hiring line.

00:06:07.690 --> 00:06:10.600
Now we're in kind of
the third AI summer.

00:06:10.600 --> 00:06:12.350
There's probably a
third AI winter coming.

00:06:12.350 --> 00:06:14.891
MELANIE WARRICK: I wouldn't be
surprised if that's happening.

00:06:14.891 --> 00:06:16.630
But I also think that
it won't completely

00:06:16.630 --> 00:06:19.360
disappear because there's so
much that's been proven out.

00:06:19.360 --> 00:06:22.330
But yes, it's nice to see
where it's at an upswing.

00:06:22.330 --> 00:06:24.940
Do you wish you could go back
and do more with it again?

00:06:24.940 --> 00:06:27.010
SAM RAMJI: You know, it's
a ridiculous privilege

00:06:27.010 --> 00:06:29.650
to be at Google because
I'm surrounded by experts

00:06:29.650 --> 00:06:30.760
in all of these fields.

00:06:30.760 --> 00:06:32.205
So do it again?

00:06:32.205 --> 00:06:33.497
Yeah, I can do that on Tuesday.

00:06:33.497 --> 00:06:34.413
MELANIE WARRICK: True.

00:06:34.413 --> 00:06:36.810
SAM RAMJI: Go talk to somebody
from Jeff Dean's team,

00:06:36.810 --> 00:06:40.330
use some of our internal
tools like our new TensorFlow

00:06:40.330 --> 00:06:42.670
automation system
and do the code lab.

00:06:42.670 --> 00:06:44.620
Last week I took a codecation.

00:06:44.620 --> 00:06:47.230
I took three work
days to learn Go

00:06:47.230 --> 00:06:49.685
and to do Kubernetes
the Hard Way on GCE.

00:06:49.685 --> 00:06:50.560
MELANIE WARRICK: Wow.

00:06:50.560 --> 00:06:52.150
SAM RAMJI: So this is a place
where you just kind of get

00:06:52.150 --> 00:06:52.870
to keep learning.

00:06:52.870 --> 00:06:54.994
The one thing I'll say is
really different about AI

00:06:54.994 --> 00:06:58.202
this time is that the scope and
scale of how it gets executed,

00:06:58.202 --> 00:07:00.410
how it's delivered, and how
it changes our experience

00:07:00.410 --> 00:07:04.720
as humans means that we really
have to be awesome at ethics.

00:07:04.720 --> 00:07:06.820
And that's something
that typically

00:07:06.820 --> 00:07:10.450
deep science and ethics have
always struggled to get along--

00:07:10.450 --> 00:07:14.200
social sciences, humanities,
and then the hard sciences,

00:07:14.200 --> 00:07:15.225
so-called.

00:07:15.225 --> 00:07:16.600
It would be really
helpful for us

00:07:16.600 --> 00:07:18.580
to all start to
find ways to be more

00:07:18.580 --> 00:07:21.460
multidisciplinary in our
team structure, more diverse

00:07:21.460 --> 00:07:24.490
in our thinking, so that we
can start to anticipate, what's

00:07:24.490 --> 00:07:27.009
the second, third,
fourth-order effects of these

00:07:27.009 --> 00:07:29.300
that aren't obvious to the
person who created the tech?

00:07:29.300 --> 00:07:32.980
So it is a major change
from '94 when I graduated.

00:07:32.980 --> 00:07:34.660
AI just didn't have
this kind of power.

00:07:34.660 --> 00:07:35.701
MELANIE WARRICK: I agree.

00:07:35.701 --> 00:07:37.410
It's definitely a huge shift.

00:07:37.410 --> 00:07:40.030
Well, so how did you
end up at Google?

00:07:40.030 --> 00:07:42.580
SAM RAMJI: That is
a great question.

00:07:42.580 --> 00:07:46.150
I always admired Google and
I thought these consumer

00:07:46.150 --> 00:07:49.070
technologies that the
company built were amazing.

00:07:49.070 --> 00:07:52.180
Obviously from search to--

00:07:52.180 --> 00:07:55.330
I moved my entire prior
startup onto Google Docs--

00:07:55.330 --> 00:07:57.070
Actually that was
two startups ago--

00:07:57.070 --> 00:07:58.810
because it was just
so efficient, again,

00:07:58.810 --> 00:08:02.324
for collaborative thinking,
how you look at what people are

00:08:02.324 --> 00:08:03.490
thinking about in real time.

00:08:03.490 --> 00:08:05.430
The closest thing you can
get is everybody's typing

00:08:05.430 --> 00:08:06.305
in the same document.

00:08:06.305 --> 00:08:07.630
It performs so well.

00:08:07.630 --> 00:08:10.080
But I'm really a plumber.

00:08:10.080 --> 00:08:11.970
I like distributed systems.

00:08:11.970 --> 00:08:14.470
And I've been in distributed
systems and enterprise software

00:08:14.470 --> 00:08:17.170
since, I don't know, 1998.

00:08:17.170 --> 00:08:19.460
So Google never
had a place for me.

00:08:19.460 --> 00:08:22.957
And so at that sort of
basic structural mismatch,

00:08:22.957 --> 00:08:25.540
I never thought, oh, Google is
a company I'd want to work for.

00:08:25.540 --> 00:08:27.220
But then a year
and a half ago when

00:08:27.220 --> 00:08:28.810
I was the CEO of
Cloud Foundry, which

00:08:28.810 --> 00:08:31.780
is the leading open source
platform as a service,

00:08:31.780 --> 00:08:33.652
I started hearing
who was at Google now

00:08:33.652 --> 00:08:35.860
and what Google cared about
and what they were doing.

00:08:35.860 --> 00:08:38.309
And I thought, this is
becoming a place for plumbers.

00:08:38.309 --> 00:08:40.980
We can really get really
excited about middleware.

00:08:40.980 --> 00:08:43.030
And we can start taking
these developer platforms

00:08:43.030 --> 00:08:44.488
and infrastructures
that Google has

00:08:44.488 --> 00:08:46.840
built for itself that
have scaled the business,

00:08:46.840 --> 00:08:49.180
made it the second-most
valuable company in the world,

00:08:49.180 --> 00:08:52.150
and start to offer that out
to every company in the world.

00:08:52.150 --> 00:08:54.057
And for me that's,
speaking of ethics,

00:08:54.057 --> 00:08:56.140
that's kind of where my
ethics have always leaned.

00:08:56.140 --> 00:08:58.780
How do we build tools that
enable other people to take

00:08:58.780 --> 00:09:00.080
care of what they care about?

00:09:00.080 --> 00:09:05.440
MARK MANDEL: So I guess you're
the VP of Product Management.

00:09:05.440 --> 00:09:08.080
What is a day in the life of a
VP of Product Management look

00:09:08.080 --> 00:09:09.682
like now that you're at Google?

00:09:09.682 --> 00:09:11.140
SAM RAMJI: Well,
it's pretty great.

00:09:11.140 --> 00:09:13.570
First of all, I think one of
the great things about Google

00:09:13.570 --> 00:09:15.111
is it's a bit of a
Montessori school,

00:09:15.111 --> 00:09:17.202
so there's an
inversion of control.

00:09:17.202 --> 00:09:18.910
The most important
person in a Montessori

00:09:18.910 --> 00:09:20.492
is the student, not the teacher.

00:09:20.492 --> 00:09:22.450
And I think at Google
the most important people

00:09:22.450 --> 00:09:25.510
are the people who are
actually doing the work, not

00:09:25.510 --> 00:09:26.380
the managers.

00:09:26.380 --> 00:09:28.990
And that's consistent from what
I've heard from Google culture

00:09:28.990 --> 00:09:30.580
over the last 19 years.

00:09:30.580 --> 00:09:32.950
So it doesn't feel very
much like, oh, you know,

00:09:32.950 --> 00:09:33.910
I'm a senior executive.

00:09:33.910 --> 00:09:35.993
And thank goodness, because
I was born in Berkeley

00:09:35.993 --> 00:09:37.600
and raised in
Oakland, so I'm pretty

00:09:37.600 --> 00:09:40.910
antihierarchical and
antiauthoritarian.

00:09:40.910 --> 00:09:43.990
So day in the life,
email, review some stuff,

00:09:43.990 --> 00:09:46.510
get your mind blown
because Jeff Dean just

00:09:46.510 --> 00:09:49.000
did a talk at NIPS
on how you can create

00:09:49.000 --> 00:09:51.960
next-generation
high-performance computer

00:09:51.960 --> 00:09:55.930
indexes via machine learning and
not classical data structures.

00:09:55.930 --> 00:09:57.530
Deal with
prioritization-- what are

00:09:57.530 --> 00:09:59.530
we going to ship, what
are we not going to ship?

00:09:59.530 --> 00:10:00.863
Where do we apply the headcount?

00:10:00.863 --> 00:10:02.834
And then what's
open-source strategy mean,

00:10:02.834 --> 00:10:04.500
where there's a lot
of opinions on that,

00:10:04.500 --> 00:10:06.749
but how do we start to apply
that since open source is

00:10:06.749 --> 00:10:09.210
so meaningful to us
and it's at scale?

00:10:09.210 --> 00:10:10.460
But should we do this or that?

00:10:10.460 --> 00:10:12.110
And is that 10 engineers or 20?

00:10:12.110 --> 00:10:14.750
And what's the
opportunity cost there?

00:10:14.750 --> 00:10:17.510
And then, hey, how do we
go and talk about this?

00:10:17.510 --> 00:10:20.249
So we got a conference schedule
coming up for this year.

00:10:20.249 --> 00:10:22.040
What are we going to
be ready to talk about

00:10:22.040 --> 00:10:23.873
and how can we use other
people's heartbeats

00:10:23.873 --> 00:10:27.590
well to stay both on
message and empirical?

00:10:27.590 --> 00:10:29.120
What have we learned
and therefore

00:10:29.120 --> 00:10:32.750
do we want to offer you from
our learning-- not theoretical

00:10:32.750 --> 00:10:35.510
and certainly keeping
it honest and humble.

00:10:35.510 --> 00:10:37.700
So it's a pretty great job.

00:10:37.700 --> 00:10:41.630
I think any job has
got its share of horses

00:10:41.630 --> 00:10:43.700
and mucking out the stable.

00:10:43.700 --> 00:10:43.760
MELANIE WARRICK: It's true.

00:10:43.760 --> 00:10:45.240
SAM RAMJI: We want
find the right balance.

00:10:45.240 --> 00:10:45.860
MELANIE WARRICK: It's very true.

00:10:45.860 --> 00:10:48.276
SAM RAMJI: But we muck out
stables because we love horses.

00:10:48.276 --> 00:10:49.790
So I think this
is the place where

00:10:49.790 --> 00:10:51.620
the things that I
really care about

00:10:51.620 --> 00:10:53.884
and the people that
I want to work with--

00:10:53.884 --> 00:10:55.550
super nerdy people
who are like, hey, it

00:10:55.550 --> 00:10:57.175
makes you more credible
as an executive

00:10:57.175 --> 00:10:58.660
that you're also
a dungeon master.

00:10:58.660 --> 00:10:59.522
I'm like--

00:10:59.522 --> 00:11:00.237
[LAUGHTER]

00:11:00.237 --> 00:11:02.570
Not that many companies are,
like, yeah, well, of course

00:11:02.570 --> 00:11:03.939
you play D&amp;D. We all do.

00:11:03.939 --> 00:11:05.730
MELANIE WARRICK: It's
very acceptable here.

00:11:05.730 --> 00:11:06.860
So in terms of--

00:11:06.860 --> 00:11:09.300
it sounds very clear
to me why you've

00:11:09.300 --> 00:11:11.300
got your background, how
you ended up definitely

00:11:11.300 --> 00:11:12.540
involved in open source.

00:11:12.540 --> 00:11:14.330
What do you miss
about Cloud Foundry?

00:11:14.330 --> 00:11:17.540
SAM RAMJI: I miss the
interaction with the board.

00:11:17.540 --> 00:11:21.020
So it's interesting to
be a CEO in a company

00:11:21.020 --> 00:11:24.500
where you were constructing,
working with senior vice

00:11:24.500 --> 00:11:27.530
presidents, CEOs
of other companies

00:11:27.530 --> 00:11:30.470
who really only have an hour
a month to hang out with you.

00:11:30.470 --> 00:11:33.480
And they know that we're
all going in the same way

00:11:33.480 --> 00:11:35.600
and they expect you to
make all the decisions.

00:11:35.600 --> 00:11:37.281
You've got the entire scope.

00:11:37.281 --> 00:11:39.780
If it fails, it's your fault.
If you succeed, it's the team.

00:11:39.780 --> 00:11:43.010
But I never had a performance
review because it was either

00:11:43.010 --> 00:11:46.180
the company was doing
well or it wasn't, and we

00:11:46.180 --> 00:11:47.430
needed to adjust those things.

00:11:47.430 --> 00:11:50.060
So there was no layer of ego.

00:11:50.060 --> 00:11:51.620
Everything was just a thing.

00:11:51.620 --> 00:11:55.190
And so getting into a mode where
we start managing and judging

00:11:55.190 --> 00:11:58.340
people and justifying our
existence as opposed to going,

00:11:58.340 --> 00:12:00.620
gosh, I'm putting
every waking heartbeat

00:12:00.620 --> 00:12:01.820
into making this great.

00:12:01.820 --> 00:12:03.780
And we're all pretty
good at this stuff,

00:12:03.780 --> 00:12:06.764
so let's get some
feedback on how to tune.

00:12:06.764 --> 00:12:08.930
But do we need to do
calibrations on all this stuff?

00:12:08.930 --> 00:12:11.150
So it's interesting to get
back into a large company

00:12:11.150 --> 00:12:14.180
because I was at
Microsoft in the 2000s.

00:12:14.180 --> 00:12:16.806
At that point, you know, many
tens of thousands of people.

00:12:16.806 --> 00:12:18.680
When I left, I think it
was 90,000 employees.

00:12:18.680 --> 00:12:20.880
So you have to have
these systems in place.

00:12:20.880 --> 00:12:22.880
But that shift from
constantly being

00:12:22.880 --> 00:12:26.300
in the doing to also having
to come back and assess

00:12:26.300 --> 00:12:29.719
and sort of justify existence,
it's an emotional adjustment.

00:12:29.719 --> 00:12:31.760
MARK MANDEL: So now I'm
actually kind of curious.

00:12:31.760 --> 00:12:34.134
Now that you've come to Google--
and I love that metaphor

00:12:34.134 --> 00:12:35.070
of Montessori school.

00:12:35.070 --> 00:12:37.500
I think that's spot
on for how Google is.

00:12:37.500 --> 00:12:40.550
SAM RAMJI: That was given to me
by a friend, so I credit him.

00:12:40.550 --> 00:12:41.240
MARK MANDEL: That is spot on.

00:12:41.240 --> 00:12:42.410
But I'm actually
kind of curious,

00:12:42.410 --> 00:12:44.060
what's been the most
surprising thing for you

00:12:44.060 --> 00:12:46.500
coming to work with Google
or Google Cloud specifically?

00:12:46.500 --> 00:12:48.860
SAM RAMJI: God, how
nice people are.

00:12:48.860 --> 00:12:50.900
I anticipated that a
little bit on the way in,

00:12:50.900 --> 00:12:53.930
but I think it also
surprised me based

00:12:53.930 --> 00:12:57.230
on my prior sense of
Google because certainly,

00:12:57.230 --> 00:12:58.670
for example, before
this podcast,

00:12:58.670 --> 00:13:01.370
it was very hard to understand
what Google people were

00:13:01.370 --> 00:13:04.070
like because the
company's ethos was like,

00:13:04.070 --> 00:13:07.442
you'll encounter us
through our web interfaces.

00:13:07.442 --> 00:13:08.900
And we can make up
a lot of stories

00:13:08.900 --> 00:13:13.970
like remote, hard to deal
with, superior, maybe.

00:13:13.970 --> 00:13:15.920
And then you actually
get into these things.

00:13:15.920 --> 00:13:18.440
One of the things I get to
do as a product manager is I

00:13:18.440 --> 00:13:21.860
get to do product management
for Eric Brewer, the person who

00:13:21.860 --> 00:13:22.880
authored CAP theorem.

00:13:22.880 --> 00:13:26.750
And students in his classes
invented things like Databricks

00:13:26.750 --> 00:13:28.310
as a side project--

00:13:28.310 --> 00:13:30.980
amazingly cool, super humble.

00:13:30.980 --> 00:13:33.260
You're just encountering
everybody's sort

00:13:33.260 --> 00:13:36.525
at the first order
of experience.

00:13:36.525 --> 00:13:38.900
You're not encountering what
they think about themselves.

00:13:38.900 --> 00:13:39.710
You're encountering
what everybody

00:13:39.710 --> 00:13:40.793
thinks about the problems.

00:13:40.793 --> 00:13:43.970
And it's a wonderful
surprise, and it

00:13:43.970 --> 00:13:46.220
catches me off guard
still every single day,

00:13:46.220 --> 00:13:48.980
because so much of the
industry in my experience

00:13:48.980 --> 00:13:50.450
hasn't been like that.

00:13:50.450 --> 00:13:52.760
Don't you know who I am?

00:13:52.760 --> 00:13:55.430
Ladies and gentlemen, this
person doesn't know who he is.

00:13:55.430 --> 00:13:57.894
It's really nice to find people
who are just into the work

00:13:57.894 --> 00:14:00.060
no matter what they've done
and just moving forward.

00:14:00.060 --> 00:14:02.240
So really nice,
super smart people

00:14:02.240 --> 00:14:05.630
with a lot of emotional
intelligence, and that's

00:14:05.630 --> 00:14:06.260
a pleasure.

00:14:06.260 --> 00:14:06.860
MELANIE WARRICK:
Well, clearly you've

00:14:06.860 --> 00:14:08.330
had very little experience.

00:14:08.330 --> 00:14:12.730
So I'm curious, where do you
want to go with your career?

00:14:12.730 --> 00:14:15.050
What gets you excited
to learn about?

00:14:15.050 --> 00:14:17.390
SAM RAMJI: Oh, two
great questions.

00:14:17.390 --> 00:14:20.630
I think there's a great quote
from the founder of Aikido

00:14:20.630 --> 00:14:22.570
which is a relatively
young martial art.

00:14:22.570 --> 00:14:25.100
His name is Morihei Ueshiba.

00:14:25.100 --> 00:14:27.166
People call Osensei.

00:14:27.166 --> 00:14:30.200
He said, "True victory is
victory over the self."

00:14:30.200 --> 00:14:32.120
So in terms of learning,
one of the things

00:14:32.120 --> 00:14:35.110
that I want to continue to do
is learn, how can I be better?

00:14:35.110 --> 00:14:37.340
There's some challenges
around, how do

00:14:37.340 --> 00:14:39.140
you do scalable communication?

00:14:39.140 --> 00:14:41.660
I speak from the heart,
got relatively good

00:14:41.660 --> 00:14:42.830
values and ethics.

00:14:42.830 --> 00:14:45.040
I can still make mistakes
in spoken language

00:14:45.040 --> 00:14:47.360
when I'm talking
with 500 people,

00:14:47.360 --> 00:14:48.920
and somebody's going
to get offended.

00:14:48.920 --> 00:14:49.677
And that's harm.

00:14:49.677 --> 00:14:50.760
And I feel bad about that.

00:14:50.760 --> 00:14:53.460
But the feedback is like,
OK, I can do better at that.

00:14:53.460 --> 00:14:55.770
So a lot of the
learning is just how

00:14:55.770 --> 00:14:59.840
to take care of large groups
of people in communication

00:14:59.840 --> 00:15:02.180
and understand, how can
I provide a little bit

00:15:02.180 --> 00:15:04.440
of input and guidance
that will open up

00:15:04.440 --> 00:15:07.380
something useful to them that
will generally get us moving

00:15:07.380 --> 00:15:08.700
in a common direction?

00:15:08.700 --> 00:15:12.810
Intense and difficult
skill, and one

00:15:12.810 --> 00:15:15.294
that you can only learn when
you're in a position where

00:15:15.294 --> 00:15:15.960
that's your job.

00:15:15.960 --> 00:15:18.060
You kind of can't
do this in school.

00:15:18.060 --> 00:15:18.810
MELANIE WARRICK: You're
at the heart of it,

00:15:18.810 --> 00:15:21.430
always thinking of distributed
computing in some way, shape,

00:15:21.430 --> 00:15:22.030
or form.

00:15:22.030 --> 00:15:22.785
SAM RAMJI: But that's
the interesting thing.

00:15:22.785 --> 00:15:24.969
It's like rather than
distributed computation,

00:15:24.969 --> 00:15:26.760
distributed cognition
is really interesting

00:15:26.760 --> 00:15:31.860
because we're the people
doing the cognitive work.

00:15:31.860 --> 00:15:33.420
We're not just
doing computation.

00:15:33.420 --> 00:15:34.560
There is emotional labor.

00:15:34.560 --> 00:15:36.300
There's a bunch of
other things going on.

00:15:36.300 --> 00:15:37.280
So how do we do that stuff?

00:15:37.280 --> 00:15:38.363
That's the learning front.

00:15:38.363 --> 00:15:42.040
I think in terms of what I want
to do, for the next decade,

00:15:42.040 --> 00:15:46.290
I would like to help Google take
what it's reinvented in what we

00:15:46.290 --> 00:15:50.730
now call DevOps and CICD
and next-generation static

00:15:50.730 --> 00:15:54.780
analysis, monitoring operations,
site-reliability engineering,

00:15:54.780 --> 00:15:58.050
this whole sort
of soup of words--

00:15:58.050 --> 00:16:00.180
what it is is a
digital operating model

00:16:00.180 --> 00:16:04.380
that has allowed Google to
create a great advertising

00:16:04.380 --> 00:16:07.710
business because it can
iterate everything about it

00:16:07.710 --> 00:16:08.520
many times a day.

00:16:08.520 --> 00:16:10.950
I think we ship to production
about 9,000 times a day.

00:16:10.950 --> 00:16:12.690
But again, not to get
distracted by what

00:16:12.690 --> 00:16:15.840
does it take to ship 9,000 times
a day but why would you do it,

00:16:15.840 --> 00:16:18.810
because you can be in this
very tight feedback loop

00:16:18.810 --> 00:16:20.877
where you're constantly
learning from your users.

00:16:20.877 --> 00:16:22.710
And that's true regardless
of your industry.

00:16:22.710 --> 00:16:23.970
We're in search and ads.

00:16:23.970 --> 00:16:25.800
Spotify's in music.

00:16:25.800 --> 00:16:28.170
They are iterating
their environment.

00:16:28.170 --> 00:16:31.070
I heard they ship to production
like 3,000 times a day.

00:16:31.070 --> 00:16:32.040
MELANIE WARRICK: Wow.

00:16:32.040 --> 00:16:34.123
SAM RAMJI: So what is that
digital operating model

00:16:34.123 --> 00:16:38.130
and how do we make that real for
the entire world, no matter how

00:16:38.130 --> 00:16:40.770
small of a company, how
new you are to software,

00:16:40.770 --> 00:16:43.115
how large of a company,
how sophisticated you are?

00:16:43.115 --> 00:16:44.490
How do we take
what we've learned

00:16:44.490 --> 00:16:47.640
and offer that as a
platform for the planet

00:16:47.640 --> 00:16:49.980
to compute how they
might care, too?

00:16:49.980 --> 00:16:53.460
MARK MANDEL: Are there
particular technical products

00:16:53.460 --> 00:16:56.190
that we have or potentially may
have in the future that get you

00:16:56.190 --> 00:16:56.930
really excited?

00:16:56.930 --> 00:16:59.280
Anything you're particularly
passionate about right now?

00:16:59.280 --> 00:17:00.132
SAM RAMJI: Oh, boy.

00:17:00.132 --> 00:17:01.590
MARK MANDEL: I
mean, just pick one.

00:17:01.590 --> 00:17:03.173
SAM RAMJI: I was
going to say, clearly

00:17:03.173 --> 00:17:04.839
I'm not a very
passionate person.

00:17:04.839 --> 00:17:07.510
I'm really lucky, I get to care
about all the things I'm doing.

00:17:07.510 --> 00:17:09.676
There's a lot of really
interesting things happening

00:17:09.676 --> 00:17:12.690
in terms of how do we take
code, which is a developer's

00:17:12.690 --> 00:17:16.980
set of intents, and turn
it into running production

00:17:16.980 --> 00:17:21.150
systems that developers and
operators can both collaborate

00:17:21.150 --> 00:17:21.700
on.

00:17:21.700 --> 00:17:23.730
There's a whole chain
of technologies there,

00:17:23.730 --> 00:17:26.220
both first-party technologies
and third-party technologies,

00:17:26.220 --> 00:17:28.530
third party like Spinnaker
that we've gotten into.

00:17:28.530 --> 00:17:30.870
It's an open-source project
that was started by Netflix.

00:17:30.870 --> 00:17:32.495
And we've gotten
really involved in it.

00:17:32.495 --> 00:17:34.980
It's a really nice way to
do multicloud computing.

00:17:34.980 --> 00:17:36.510
And all of these
things really need

00:17:36.510 --> 00:17:38.310
to come back to
giving developers

00:17:38.310 --> 00:17:40.710
control of exactly how
they want their code turned

00:17:40.710 --> 00:17:42.630
into an artifact
like a container,

00:17:42.630 --> 00:17:46.261
how they want to structure
it into services and pods,

00:17:46.261 --> 00:17:47.760
and where they might
want to run it.

00:17:47.760 --> 00:17:49.593
So I think part of what
brought me to Google

00:17:49.593 --> 00:17:52.020
was this core belief
in open hybrid cloud.

00:17:52.020 --> 00:17:54.120
When I left Cloud
Foundry, I had spent

00:17:54.120 --> 00:17:56.430
two years committing
all of my heartbeats

00:17:56.430 --> 00:17:59.654
to putting technology
back under the control

00:17:59.654 --> 00:18:01.320
of the companies that
use it rather than

00:18:01.320 --> 00:18:02.657
the companies that sell it.

00:18:02.657 --> 00:18:04.740
And part of what brought
me here was Brian Stevens

00:18:04.740 --> 00:18:07.497
said, "Our mission is
to be the open cloud."

00:18:07.497 --> 00:18:08.830
I said, "You must be kidding me.

00:18:08.830 --> 00:18:10.470
That doesn't make any sense.

00:18:10.470 --> 00:18:12.750
Every hyperscale cloud
provider is clearly

00:18:12.750 --> 00:18:14.880
an ambitious monopolist."

00:18:14.880 --> 00:18:15.870
Not at all, right?

00:18:15.870 --> 00:18:18.810
So when we look at this,
we say your computation

00:18:18.810 --> 00:18:20.447
should be wherever it is.

00:18:20.447 --> 00:18:22.030
That's a really hard
problem to solve.

00:18:22.030 --> 00:18:24.120
How do we give you
a control plane that

00:18:24.120 --> 00:18:26.500
lets you build your
code however you want,

00:18:26.500 --> 00:18:28.620
deploy it however
you want, including

00:18:28.620 --> 00:18:32.160
to multiple environments on
premises, multiple clouds,

00:18:32.160 --> 00:18:34.350
and then keep track of
how it's all working?

00:18:34.350 --> 00:18:36.540
So that's really
soaking up my attention

00:18:36.540 --> 00:18:38.970
because I think so many
things are moving fast

00:18:38.970 --> 00:18:39.930
but better understood.

00:18:39.930 --> 00:18:42.690
Like in infrastructure service,
which we do a great job of,

00:18:42.690 --> 00:18:45.330
we're continuing to add
things like tensor processing

00:18:45.330 --> 00:18:49.130
units, 180 teraflops of
compute on a single board.

00:18:49.130 --> 00:18:50.880
Just kind of mind
blowing, and that's just

00:18:50.880 --> 00:18:51.600
the current generation.

00:18:51.600 --> 00:18:52.530
MELANIE WARRICK:
Yeah, it's fantastic.

00:18:52.530 --> 00:18:54.030
SAM RAMJI: Our
platform as a service

00:18:54.030 --> 00:18:56.400
work we're doing around
App Engine, serverless,

00:18:56.400 --> 00:18:59.820
Google Kubernetes Engine,
our cloud services platform.

00:18:59.820 --> 00:19:02.370
Those things are all
fascinating, better understood,

00:19:02.370 --> 00:19:04.320
and moving quickly.

00:19:04.320 --> 00:19:07.530
This slightly-- maybe we'll
use the word inchoate,

00:19:07.530 --> 00:19:10.110
it's not completely formed--

00:19:10.110 --> 00:19:13.330
world of, I'm a developer
and I'm writing code.

00:19:13.330 --> 00:19:15.330
How do we make sure
the correct code that's

00:19:15.330 --> 00:19:17.490
scalable and optimized
gets to production

00:19:17.490 --> 00:19:22.200
and can be iterated fast by a
distributed, diverse, inclusive

00:19:22.200 --> 00:19:25.590
team of people to make sure
that the whole thing works?

00:19:25.590 --> 00:19:26.917
That's fascinating to me.

00:19:26.917 --> 00:19:28.110
MELANIE WARRICK: Nice.

00:19:28.110 --> 00:19:30.420
Do you remember,
when you were coding,

00:19:30.420 --> 00:19:33.300
what you enjoyed the
most about writing code?

00:19:33.300 --> 00:19:35.160
SAM RAMJI: Focus.

00:19:35.160 --> 00:19:36.750
MARK MANDEL: Just one thing?

00:19:36.750 --> 00:19:40.100
SAM RAMJI: Just such a
visceral pleasure, right,

00:19:40.100 --> 00:19:41.550
just being able to sit down.

00:19:41.550 --> 00:19:44.940
And as developers, I was
doing most of my code

00:19:44.940 --> 00:19:46.920
in the '90s on C++.

00:19:46.920 --> 00:19:49.020
And you're working
your brain out.

00:19:49.020 --> 00:19:51.180
You're working 12,
14 hours a day.

00:19:51.180 --> 00:19:53.070
And there's actually
a deep pleasure

00:19:53.070 --> 00:19:55.890
in being able to focus your
brain for that much time.

00:19:55.890 --> 00:19:59.160
Put on your headphones, put
the football on your computer

00:19:59.160 --> 00:20:00.780
monitor that says
don't interrupt me

00:20:00.780 --> 00:20:02.220
because it's neon
orange and everybody's

00:20:02.220 --> 00:20:03.610
going to know to
leave you alone,

00:20:03.610 --> 00:20:04.780
whatever those things are.

00:20:04.780 --> 00:20:07.321
And there's a real satisfaction
in getting your brain so deep

00:20:07.321 --> 00:20:10.210
in something and at the
end, compiles, runs.

00:20:10.210 --> 00:20:10.960
It works.

00:20:10.960 --> 00:20:11.832
It's bug free.

00:20:11.832 --> 00:20:13.540
You've kind of done
the right design work

00:20:13.540 --> 00:20:14.873
and everything has come through.

00:20:14.873 --> 00:20:17.440
Or you end up with a gnarly
bug and you're like, oh,

00:20:17.440 --> 00:20:20.552
my God, what the hell is
happening with memory?

00:20:20.552 --> 00:20:22.510
These really interesting
intellectual problems,

00:20:22.510 --> 00:20:24.610
you're kind of constantly
doing science on your own work.

00:20:24.610 --> 00:20:25.660
And you're constantly learning.

00:20:25.660 --> 00:20:27.160
So there's a lot
of things about it.

00:20:27.160 --> 00:20:28.690
But the one thing, just focus.

00:20:28.690 --> 00:20:31.650
If you're a happy programmer,
it's because you're focusing.

00:20:31.650 --> 00:20:33.940
And if you're unhappy, it's
because product managers

00:20:33.940 --> 00:20:35.231
are bothering you all the time.

00:20:35.231 --> 00:20:36.490
MELANIE WARRICK: It's true.

00:20:36.490 --> 00:20:38.510
MARK MANDEL: You seem quite
passionate about open source,

00:20:38.510 --> 00:20:40.960
which common passion I think
of both of ours, and probably

00:20:40.960 --> 00:20:42.560
very many people here
inside Google especially.

00:20:42.560 --> 00:20:44.080
SAM RAMJI: It does seem
to be like a particularly

00:20:44.080 --> 00:20:45.260
Google-y attribute.

00:20:45.260 --> 00:20:47.614
There's a thing
that just whatever

00:20:47.614 --> 00:20:49.030
you happen to be
doing, the ethics

00:20:49.030 --> 00:20:52.300
seem to be around openness
and end-user control.

00:20:52.300 --> 00:20:55.450
And so you look at YouTube,
broadcast yourself.

00:20:55.450 --> 00:20:57.460
Open source is kind of
like for programmers,

00:20:57.460 --> 00:21:00.251
like broadcast
yourself, and borrow.

00:21:00.251 --> 00:21:01.000
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:21:01.000 --> 00:21:02.560
But I think it's
interesting to look

00:21:02.560 --> 00:21:04.090
at the landscape of open source.

00:21:04.090 --> 00:21:04.940
Like I almost think
back-- you want

00:21:04.940 --> 00:21:06.904
to go back to the '90s
when it was shareware.

00:21:06.904 --> 00:21:08.070
That's even pre-open source.

00:21:08.070 --> 00:21:11.260
But how that landscape has
changed over even the last 10

00:21:11.260 --> 00:21:13.510
years, I'm sure you have
opinions about things

00:21:13.510 --> 00:21:16.570
like that, about how open
source has really almost become

00:21:16.570 --> 00:21:18.190
a corporate thing in many ways.

00:21:18.190 --> 00:21:19.606
SAM RAMJI: It's
interesting, there

00:21:19.606 --> 00:21:22.480
was a meet up in, I
want to say, 2006,

00:21:22.480 --> 00:21:25.240
2007 called the Open
Source Think Tank.

00:21:25.240 --> 00:21:29.500
And there were several dozen
open source technologists,

00:21:29.500 --> 00:21:30.400
business leaders.

00:21:30.400 --> 00:21:33.820
And one of the sessions was a
challenge, a working session

00:21:33.820 --> 00:21:35.680
for the whole group to
say, what do you want

00:21:35.680 --> 00:21:37.330
the brand of open source to be?

00:21:37.330 --> 00:21:40.150
And I ended up at a table
where one of these folks

00:21:40.150 --> 00:21:42.820
I won't name, who was a
respected open-source CEO

00:21:42.820 --> 00:21:45.244
at the time, he said,
"Open source should

00:21:45.244 --> 00:21:46.285
stand for revolutionary."

00:21:46.285 --> 00:21:49.660
And I said, I respectfully
completely disagree.

00:21:49.660 --> 00:21:51.280
Every revolution,
if it's successful,

00:21:51.280 --> 00:21:53.980
becomes the establishment.

00:21:53.980 --> 00:21:56.350
So that's where we're at, right?

00:21:56.350 --> 00:21:59.000
You play your kids Bob Dylan
and they're not like, oh man,

00:21:59.000 --> 00:22:00.470
that's so hot and revolutionary.

00:22:00.470 --> 00:22:03.130
They're like, oh man,
who's that old guy?

00:22:03.130 --> 00:22:05.140
I heard that music growing up.

00:22:05.140 --> 00:22:08.320
So open source, I think, is
at that point on the track.

00:22:08.320 --> 00:22:11.470
It is a basic expectation,
the core licensing

00:22:11.470 --> 00:22:12.920
model for developers.

00:22:12.920 --> 00:22:14.920
The fascinating thing I
think that it represents

00:22:14.920 --> 00:22:18.340
is actually the shift from
artisanal or craft level

00:22:18.340 --> 00:22:21.699
to more industrial-scale
use of software.

00:22:21.699 --> 00:22:23.740
If you look at what's
happened in every industry,

00:22:23.740 --> 00:22:26.830
the very first machines built
in the Industrial Revolution

00:22:26.830 --> 00:22:29.830
weren't using standard
fasteners or standard tools.

00:22:29.830 --> 00:22:31.520
You look at the
construction industry,

00:22:31.520 --> 00:22:34.600
people were using
bespoke pieces of wood.

00:22:34.600 --> 00:22:36.550
When you get to two
by fours and you

00:22:36.550 --> 00:22:40.582
get to eighth-inch precision
on the nails and fasteners

00:22:40.582 --> 00:22:42.790
you're using, you start to
standardize the profession

00:22:42.790 --> 00:22:44.710
and it actually
expands dramatically.

00:22:44.710 --> 00:22:47.320
So I see open source as
being the standardization,

00:22:47.320 --> 00:22:49.870
even though the standards
kind of keep moving.

00:22:49.870 --> 00:22:52.960
You're not trying to guess what
some weird proprietary thing

00:22:52.960 --> 00:22:53.584
is.

00:22:53.584 --> 00:22:55.000
You're like, well,
of course we're

00:22:55.000 --> 00:22:56.900
going to use Node.js, right?

00:22:56.900 --> 00:22:59.380
There's a bunch of standards
you can assume that you're

00:22:59.380 --> 00:23:00.880
going to use in any case.

00:23:00.880 --> 00:23:03.400
And that enables us to do
more professionalization, more

00:23:03.400 --> 00:23:04.504
industrialization.

00:23:04.504 --> 00:23:06.670
And I think we'll probably
see, over the next couple

00:23:06.670 --> 00:23:08.470
of decades, 10 times
more developers

00:23:08.470 --> 00:23:11.860
than we have today, just as you
will see any industry expand

00:23:11.860 --> 00:23:14.650
based on better common
standards, better

00:23:14.650 --> 00:23:16.310
understanding of what
value looks like,

00:23:16.310 --> 00:23:19.630
and a more predictable path
from, I have an idea to, I

00:23:19.630 --> 00:23:20.560
have a result.

00:23:20.560 --> 00:23:21.976
MELANIE WARRICK:
I'm curious, too,

00:23:21.976 --> 00:23:24.400
if you think where
things are going if--

00:23:24.400 --> 00:23:26.067
education has been
evolving to a degree.

00:23:26.067 --> 00:23:27.816
Do you think that
that's going to continue

00:23:27.816 --> 00:23:29.680
to keep shifting and
changing, especially

00:23:29.680 --> 00:23:32.080
in the tech side of things,
especially from a computer

00:23:32.080 --> 00:23:34.722
science-developer perspective?

00:23:34.722 --> 00:23:36.180
SAM RAMJI: It feels
like it has to.

00:23:36.180 --> 00:23:39.280
I think so much of our
education system now still--

00:23:39.280 --> 00:23:41.650
and I'm not speaking out
of personal knowledge

00:23:41.650 --> 00:23:44.320
or experience, so maybe I'm
being an internet professor.

00:23:44.320 --> 00:23:46.690
My wife was a sixth-grade
teacher for about six years,

00:23:46.690 --> 00:23:48.421
so I've learned a bit from her.

00:23:48.421 --> 00:23:49.920
Much of our current
education system

00:23:49.920 --> 00:23:52.750
is built on the
US Army's insights

00:23:52.750 --> 00:23:56.430
in the early 1900s about
how to sort of induct

00:23:56.430 --> 00:24:00.040
and indoctrinate and assess
tens or hundreds of thousands

00:24:00.040 --> 00:24:01.750
of people at a time,
and then to get

00:24:01.750 --> 00:24:04.270
them to produce predictable
results by standardizing

00:24:04.270 --> 00:24:06.070
the process.

00:24:06.070 --> 00:24:08.800
That seems pretty different
from the world we're in today.

00:24:08.800 --> 00:24:12.460
So is education in that
style really helping us

00:24:12.460 --> 00:24:14.292
deal with a world that
is changing faster?

00:24:14.292 --> 00:24:16.750
I don't think it's just because
we're getting older that we

00:24:16.750 --> 00:24:17.916
feel like it's going faster.

00:24:17.916 --> 00:24:19.240
Measurably it's getting faster.

00:24:19.240 --> 00:24:22.270
And organizations are having
a harder time adapting to it--

00:24:22.270 --> 00:24:24.850
so a more adaptive
education system

00:24:24.850 --> 00:24:26.590
that teaches us how to learn.

00:24:26.590 --> 00:24:28.570
Alvin Toffler wrote
a book in the 1980s

00:24:28.570 --> 00:24:29.560
called "Future Shock."

00:24:29.560 --> 00:24:31.320
And there's an
outstanding quote that

00:24:31.320 --> 00:24:32.740
has crystallized my thinking.

00:24:32.740 --> 00:24:36.324
He says, "The illiterate of the
future will not be those who

00:24:36.324 --> 00:24:37.240
cannot read and write.

00:24:37.240 --> 00:24:39.930
The illiterate of the future
will be those who cannot learn

00:24:39.930 --> 00:24:41.170
and unlearn."

00:24:41.170 --> 00:24:43.870
So how are we
getting our society

00:24:43.870 --> 00:24:45.880
ready for the 21st
century by teaching people

00:24:45.880 --> 00:24:47.800
that knowledge is
actually ephemeral?

00:24:47.800 --> 00:24:49.870
You can kind of page
it in and page it out.

00:24:49.870 --> 00:24:52.510
It's the structure of knowing
what to know and how to know it

00:24:52.510 --> 00:24:54.110
that are the important skills.

00:24:54.110 --> 00:24:55.930
How are we enabling
people to do that?

00:24:55.930 --> 00:24:58.750
Certainly programmers know this.

00:24:58.750 --> 00:25:01.220
This is something that's
been core to our industry.

00:25:01.220 --> 00:25:03.710
So what do we do to
make ourselves better

00:25:03.710 --> 00:25:05.210
and what can we
learn from how we've

00:25:05.210 --> 00:25:08.275
learned to help other
industries make that same leap?

00:25:08.275 --> 00:25:09.650
MARK MANDEL: I'm
curious, though,

00:25:09.650 --> 00:25:12.410
as well, that sounds like
the future education-wise,

00:25:12.410 --> 00:25:14.030
but technologically-wise,
do you think

00:25:14.030 --> 00:25:17.030
there are particular
challenges on the horizon?

00:25:17.030 --> 00:25:18.740
You're talking more
about standardization

00:25:18.740 --> 00:25:20.660
around technical models, but
there could also potentially

00:25:20.660 --> 00:25:22.494
be diversification as
new things come up.

00:25:22.494 --> 00:25:24.410
How do you think we're
going to kind of combat

00:25:24.410 --> 00:25:25.993
that sort of ebb and
flow of that kind

00:25:25.993 --> 00:25:27.080
of technological change?

00:25:27.080 --> 00:25:30.830
SAM RAMJI: Boy, can you combat
it, or can you learn to surf?

00:25:30.830 --> 00:25:32.810
I think so much of this is--

00:25:32.810 --> 00:25:35.200
and I'll make another
personal reference

00:25:35.200 --> 00:25:38.210
about a 17-year-old
Buddhist practice.

00:25:38.210 --> 00:25:40.802
And as you kind of go
through mindfulness

00:25:40.802 --> 00:25:42.260
and then you kind
of get into, what

00:25:42.260 --> 00:25:44.420
are they trying to teach
you, one of the big things

00:25:44.420 --> 00:25:45.420
is, don't cling.

00:25:45.420 --> 00:25:47.630
It's the clinging itself,
to believe that something's

00:25:47.630 --> 00:25:50.250
permanent and durable, that is
going to cause you suffering.

00:25:50.250 --> 00:25:52.280
So if we can of go,
well, things are

00:25:52.280 --> 00:25:54.560
going to be in a
constant set of change,

00:25:54.560 --> 00:25:58.280
can we teach people how to
navigate and say, actually,

00:25:58.280 --> 00:26:00.386
the next technology
looks like this?

00:26:00.386 --> 00:26:01.760
What I'd like to
see that we need

00:26:01.760 --> 00:26:04.130
to do to support
human beings in this,

00:26:04.130 --> 00:26:06.800
whether we're a large
software vendor like Google

00:26:06.800 --> 00:26:09.980
or if we're a tiny
company using that,

00:26:09.980 --> 00:26:13.910
how do you build an expectation
that, as companies, we

00:26:13.910 --> 00:26:17.720
take care of people to learn
new things and to retrain,

00:26:17.720 --> 00:26:19.970
and that we don't expect
that everybody's at 100%

00:26:19.970 --> 00:26:21.561
efficiency all the time?

00:26:21.561 --> 00:26:23.060
That we say, OK,
well, we'd like you

00:26:23.060 --> 00:26:25.520
to be at 50% efficiency for
the next three or four months

00:26:25.520 --> 00:26:28.160
as you get ready
for a leap forward

00:26:28.160 --> 00:26:30.510
by learning this new thing
because times have changed.

00:26:30.510 --> 00:26:33.120
This kind of changing the
basic ethics of what business

00:26:33.120 --> 00:26:36.200
is to continue education
in work will probably

00:26:36.200 --> 00:26:40.160
get us into a less fractious
society where we're not

00:26:40.160 --> 00:26:41.240
leaving people behind.

00:26:41.240 --> 00:26:43.610
So I tend to think
of the larger scale

00:26:43.610 --> 00:26:46.640
societal issues as
being more important

00:26:46.640 --> 00:26:48.770
because the people who
are already in tech

00:26:48.770 --> 00:26:50.130
are going to be fine.

00:26:50.130 --> 00:26:50.900
MELANIE WARRICK: Is there
anything in particular

00:26:50.900 --> 00:26:53.180
that you found that you've
clinged to in the past

00:26:53.180 --> 00:26:55.000
that you wish you
had let go of sooner?

00:26:55.000 --> 00:26:57.110
SAM RAMJI: Oh, that's
a wonderful question.

00:26:57.110 --> 00:27:00.679
I think probably
ego and identity.

00:27:00.679 --> 00:27:02.220
MELANIE WARRICK: I
was thinking tech,

00:27:02.220 --> 00:27:04.520
but this is definitely
more profound.

00:27:04.520 --> 00:27:06.560
SAM RAMJI: You know,
just like, it's

00:27:06.560 --> 00:27:08.720
easy to get mad about
a thing because you're

00:27:08.720 --> 00:27:09.800
hanging onto the idea.

00:27:09.800 --> 00:27:12.050
And then if you take
another look behind that,

00:27:12.050 --> 00:27:14.840
you're hanging onto the
idea because it was yours

00:27:14.840 --> 00:27:17.660
and it means something
about you if it is right

00:27:17.660 --> 00:27:19.610
or if it's wrong.

00:27:19.610 --> 00:27:22.010
So I think-- you know, I've
been a strategy professional

00:27:22.010 --> 00:27:23.180
for about 15 years.

00:27:23.180 --> 00:27:25.550
And going from my
early days where

00:27:25.550 --> 00:27:27.530
I was a really bad strategist--

00:27:27.530 --> 00:27:29.510
I was really hanging
on to the ideas--

00:27:29.510 --> 00:27:32.390
and these days where I'm
a less bad strategist,

00:27:32.390 --> 00:27:36.050
the ideas are a little bit
like a Lego block on the table.

00:27:36.050 --> 00:27:36.650
It's not me.

00:27:36.650 --> 00:27:38.000
It's a Lego block.

00:27:38.000 --> 00:27:39.440
Let's talk about the structure.

00:27:39.440 --> 00:27:40.460
Can it be made better?

00:27:40.460 --> 00:27:43.880
Let's attack the ideas
to improve them and not

00:27:43.880 --> 00:27:44.784
cling to them.

00:27:44.784 --> 00:27:46.700
So that was kind of a
big professional outcome

00:27:46.700 --> 00:27:49.605
from letting go of ego because
it allows us to collaborate

00:27:49.605 --> 00:27:51.230
better, because you're
not offending me

00:27:51.230 --> 00:27:52.910
when you beat up the idea.

00:27:52.910 --> 00:27:55.910
You're actually helping me and
we're all benefiting from it.

00:27:55.910 --> 00:27:57.700
MARK MANDEL: Strong
opinions weakly held?

00:27:57.700 --> 00:28:00.000
SAM RAMJI: Sure,
and scientifically.

00:28:00.000 --> 00:28:02.000
MARK MANDEL: Actually if
you want to swing back,

00:28:02.000 --> 00:28:03.230
like loose coupling, right?

00:28:03.230 --> 00:28:04.100
SAM RAMJI: For sure.

00:28:04.100 --> 00:28:05.850
MARK MANDEL: It's a
technical perspective.

00:28:05.850 --> 00:28:08.540
SAM RAMJI: Yeah, or to
quote a terrible '80s hit,

00:28:08.540 --> 00:28:10.889
hold on loosely.

00:28:10.889 --> 00:28:12.680
Now you're all thinking
about Southern rock

00:28:12.680 --> 00:28:14.151
if you share any
of my interests.

00:28:14.151 --> 00:28:16.400
MELANIE WARRICK: If you know
any of that, that's nice.

00:28:16.400 --> 00:28:18.440
MARK MANDEL: All right, let's
swing it back to Google Cloud.

00:28:18.440 --> 00:28:19.740
We are the "Google
Cloud Platform Podcast,"

00:28:19.740 --> 00:28:20.780
as much as this is wonderful.

00:28:20.780 --> 00:28:21.620
MELANIE WARRICK: We are.

00:28:21.620 --> 00:28:23.911
MARK MANDEL: I'm going to
throw the wonderful question.

00:28:23.911 --> 00:28:26.210
If you had to look
into a crystal ball,

00:28:26.210 --> 00:28:28.850
for lack of a metaphor
that's better, 5 years,

00:28:28.850 --> 00:28:30.470
10 years in the
future, where do you

00:28:30.470 --> 00:28:32.690
think this cloud
stuff is going to go?

00:28:32.690 --> 00:28:34.460
What do you see in
the future that way?

00:28:34.460 --> 00:28:36.418
SAM RAMJI: I think it's
going to become obvious

00:28:36.418 --> 00:28:39.980
that there's no manifest
destiny for cloud computing

00:28:39.980 --> 00:28:41.660
just because it
happens to be a cloud.

00:28:41.660 --> 00:28:44.030
I think you'll find that
computing is everywhere.

00:28:44.030 --> 00:28:47.330
One analogy that folks
have drawn to computing

00:28:47.330 --> 00:28:49.520
is electricity, which is
broken in a couple of ways.

00:28:49.520 --> 00:28:52.640
But the most important
failure of the analogy

00:28:52.640 --> 00:28:56.900
is, oh hey, we have
electrical utilities,

00:28:56.900 --> 00:28:58.287
so you don't need anything else.

00:28:58.287 --> 00:28:59.870
And now that you
have cloud computing,

00:28:59.870 --> 00:29:01.080
you don't need anything else.

00:29:01.080 --> 00:29:02.663
And to that I say,
please go and count

00:29:02.663 --> 00:29:04.760
the number of
generators in the world,

00:29:04.760 --> 00:29:07.220
and talk to Honda about
their generator business.

00:29:07.220 --> 00:29:08.000
It's darned good.

00:29:08.000 --> 00:29:09.500
It turns out there's
lots of reasons

00:29:09.500 --> 00:29:10.791
for computing to be everywhere.

00:29:10.791 --> 00:29:13.482
And I don't just mean
like on your watch.

00:29:13.482 --> 00:29:15.440
But actually just like
an electrical generator,

00:29:15.440 --> 00:29:17.540
a lot of reasons for the
building to have a computer.

00:29:17.540 --> 00:29:18.800
There's locality of reference.

00:29:18.800 --> 00:29:19.380
There's data.

00:29:19.380 --> 00:29:23.540
There's, oh, my God, you're on
a giant seismographic survey

00:29:23.540 --> 00:29:25.490
vessel in the
middle of the ocean,

00:29:25.490 --> 00:29:28.970
and you're collecting with
really powerful sensors

00:29:28.970 --> 00:29:30.480
petabytes a day.

00:29:30.480 --> 00:29:32.450
You're going to throw
that back to Norway

00:29:32.450 --> 00:29:35.010
for processing via satellite?

00:29:35.010 --> 00:29:35.960
Most likely not.

00:29:35.960 --> 00:29:37.610
So we'll find lots
and lots of reasons

00:29:37.610 --> 00:29:39.580
why computing
belongs everywhere.

00:29:39.580 --> 00:29:42.190
And there's no manifest destiny
to pull it onto the cloud.

00:29:42.190 --> 00:29:43.920
And I think that's critical.

00:29:43.920 --> 00:29:46.820
The other thing that's
broken about the analogy--

00:29:46.820 --> 00:29:48.840
and I admire Nick Carr.

00:29:48.840 --> 00:29:52.430
The end of IT and all of that
stuff was well thought out.

00:29:52.430 --> 00:29:55.790
I think mostly wrong
also because business

00:29:55.790 --> 00:29:56.960
is distributed cognition.

00:29:56.960 --> 00:29:58.730
Computing enables cognition.

00:29:58.730 --> 00:30:01.070
It can't actually be
commoditized in the way

00:30:01.070 --> 00:30:03.880
that electricity's
been commoditized.

00:30:03.880 --> 00:30:06.500
You want to be able to
continue to move that boundary.

00:30:06.500 --> 00:30:09.850
So I think as we continue to be
better humans, as we continue

00:30:09.850 --> 00:30:12.550
to build better organizations
that think better together,

00:30:12.550 --> 00:30:14.680
we will always find there's
something novel that we

00:30:14.680 --> 00:30:15.820
continue to seek.

00:30:15.820 --> 00:30:19.695
So your point about can you
really standardize or do

00:30:19.695 --> 00:30:21.820
we have to kind of deal
with a little bit of a mess

00:30:21.820 --> 00:30:24.040
there, I think it's
going to be messy

00:30:24.040 --> 00:30:26.975
and we'll tend to avoid
pure commoditization

00:30:26.975 --> 00:30:30.850
in the classic economic
sense for at least a decade.

00:30:30.850 --> 00:30:33.250
Some of the things that will
push it in that way will be,

00:30:33.250 --> 00:30:35.583
people are going to have
different approaches to machine

00:30:35.583 --> 00:30:37.190
learning and automation.

00:30:37.190 --> 00:30:38.880
Those opinions will
be strongly held.

00:30:38.880 --> 00:30:40.750
How you should hold
and process the data

00:30:40.750 --> 00:30:42.670
and how all that
stuff works, those

00:30:42.670 --> 00:30:45.610
will tend to push
against commoditization.

00:30:45.610 --> 00:30:47.950
MELANIE WARRICK: So a little
off the cloud for a second.

00:30:47.950 --> 00:30:51.040
I'm curious, you were talking
before about letting go of ego.

00:30:51.040 --> 00:30:53.329
I'm wondering what
you would recommend

00:30:53.329 --> 00:30:55.120
or what are some of
the key traits you find

00:30:55.120 --> 00:30:57.910
make you a successful leader or
make others successful leaders

00:30:57.910 --> 00:30:59.302
that you've worked with?

00:30:59.302 --> 00:31:00.760
SAM RAMJI: Well,
whether or not I'm

00:31:00.760 --> 00:31:04.438
a successful leader
is always debatable.

00:31:04.438 --> 00:31:06.062
MARK MANDEL: We think
you're all right.

00:31:06.062 --> 00:31:07.400
[LAUGHTER]

00:31:07.400 --> 00:31:09.190
MELANIE WARRICK: So-so.

00:31:09.190 --> 00:31:11.590
SAM RAMJI: I do think
mindfulness meditation is

00:31:11.590 --> 00:31:12.400
important.

00:31:12.400 --> 00:31:14.410
If you can't let go
of thinking, it's

00:31:14.410 --> 00:31:16.600
hard to know what you
think because you're

00:31:16.600 --> 00:31:19.150
a little bit more like
a shaken can of soda

00:31:19.150 --> 00:31:20.500
than a still glass of water.

00:31:20.500 --> 00:31:22.090
That's super important
because it also

00:31:22.090 --> 00:31:24.910
lets you kind of be
a little calm when

00:31:24.910 --> 00:31:25.870
things are triggering.

00:31:25.870 --> 00:31:27.745
One of the things that
comes up in leadership

00:31:27.745 --> 00:31:30.200
is when a leader stands
up, two things happen.

00:31:30.200 --> 00:31:32.200
One, everything that
opposes that person's point

00:31:32.200 --> 00:31:34.960
of view come into
existence that same moment.

00:31:34.960 --> 00:31:37.660
It's kind of a
philosophical construct

00:31:37.660 --> 00:31:39.500
but I've found it to be true.

00:31:39.500 --> 00:31:42.100
The other thing is, you are
the top-level exception handler

00:31:42.100 --> 00:31:45.290
for the organization,
to use a C++ reference.

00:31:45.290 --> 00:31:47.170
So if your day's
going well, you're

00:31:47.170 --> 00:31:49.240
probably getting a half
a dozen escalations

00:31:49.240 --> 00:31:52.400
that weren't part of your
originally scheduled routine.

00:31:52.400 --> 00:31:55.060
And some of those, they
might be quite banal.

00:31:55.060 --> 00:31:58.390
Sometimes they might
be quite fervent.

00:31:58.390 --> 00:32:01.630
So for you to be the one who's
calm when everybody else is

00:32:01.630 --> 00:32:04.150
excited is really important.

00:32:04.150 --> 00:32:07.600
And it's easiest and less tiring
if you're calm on the inside

00:32:07.600 --> 00:32:08.980
and on the outside.

00:32:08.980 --> 00:32:10.690
You have to be calm
on the outside.

00:32:10.690 --> 00:32:11.890
That's table stakes.

00:32:11.890 --> 00:32:14.320
But being calm on the
inside lets you kind of get

00:32:14.320 --> 00:32:15.800
through all of it.

00:32:15.800 --> 00:32:17.900
So I think just
knowing how to relax,

00:32:17.900 --> 00:32:19.690
how to take a step
back, and create

00:32:19.690 --> 00:32:21.820
a safe space for
everybody else to use

00:32:21.820 --> 00:32:23.380
their natural
human genius, which

00:32:23.380 --> 00:32:26.137
is their intelligence of how to
work through problems, that's

00:32:26.137 --> 00:32:26.720
the key thing.

00:32:26.720 --> 00:32:30.750
Sometimes things will
descend into arguments.

00:32:30.750 --> 00:32:33.540
But if you can just kind of
keep opening up a safe space,

00:32:33.540 --> 00:32:34.800
people will work it out.

00:32:34.800 --> 00:32:36.350
Everybody will feel
good at the end.

00:32:36.350 --> 00:32:37.370
They'll be thankful.

00:32:37.370 --> 00:32:40.001
And you'll be like, look,
you did it yourself.

00:32:40.001 --> 00:32:40.750
MARK MANDEL: Nice.

00:32:40.750 --> 00:32:41.874
MELANIE WARRICK: Thank you.

00:32:41.874 --> 00:32:43.920
Well, so I think
