00:34:07.300 --> 00:34:10.357
So, Mark, let's talk about
the question of the week.

00:34:10.357 --> 00:34:12.190
And as I mentioned, the
question of the week

00:34:12.190 --> 00:34:16.960
is, I want to run a Google Cloud
Function every so many day,

00:34:16.960 --> 00:34:18.320
week, whatever.

00:34:18.320 --> 00:34:19.690
But I can't do cron.

00:34:19.690 --> 00:34:21.719
How do I do that
in Cloud Functions?

00:34:21.719 --> 00:34:22.510
MARK MANDEL: Sweet.

00:34:22.510 --> 00:34:25.449
So yeah, it's unfortunate
that we don't have cron

00:34:25.449 --> 00:34:26.980
in Cloud Functions right now.

00:34:26.980 --> 00:34:28.540
But there's some
nice workarounds.

00:34:28.540 --> 00:34:30.489
There's a few different ways
I've seen people do this,

00:34:30.489 --> 00:34:32.020
but this is the one
I like the best.

00:34:32.020 --> 00:34:34.687
And it comes from our
friends over at Firebase,

00:34:34.687 --> 00:34:35.520
which is quite nice.

00:34:35.520 --> 00:34:37.540
And we'll link to the
GitHub repository in here.

00:34:37.540 --> 00:34:39.730
But basically the
short answer is this.

00:34:39.730 --> 00:34:41.679
It really requires two
things, so App Engine,

00:34:41.679 --> 00:34:43.514
which has cron
capabilities, and Pub/Sub.

00:34:43.514 --> 00:34:45.639
There is a link we will
have in the show notes that

00:34:45.639 --> 00:34:48.250
points to the GitHub
repository, but basically we

00:34:48.250 --> 00:34:51.650
set up, using this code, three
different Pub/Sub topics--

00:34:51.650 --> 00:34:53.889
so one for hourly, one for
weekly, and one for daily.

00:34:53.889 --> 00:34:56.139
You could obviously change
that to be your own things.

00:34:56.139 --> 00:34:58.150
And then we have cron
with an App Engine that

00:34:58.150 --> 00:35:00.070
just hits those and
pushes something

00:35:00.070 --> 00:35:02.750
into that topic on
that regular basis.

00:35:02.750 --> 00:35:06.230
That means then that you can
use those for any Cloud Function

00:35:06.230 --> 00:35:08.754
that you want to run on that
period, which is quite cool.

00:35:08.754 --> 00:35:09.920
So it's nice and extensible.

00:35:09.920 --> 00:35:11.570
That's why I like
this one the most,

00:35:11.570 --> 00:35:14.210
because Cloud Functions
have Pub/Sub hooks.

00:35:14.210 --> 00:35:16.170
So you can fire off a
Cloud Function whenever

00:35:16.170 --> 00:35:18.050
Pub/Sub, something
gets pushed into it

00:35:18.050 --> 00:35:19.370
and you want to react to it.

00:35:19.370 --> 00:35:21.470
And you can have
multiple Cloud Functions

00:35:21.470 --> 00:35:23.830
reacting to the same
message, going through--

00:35:23.830 --> 00:35:25.330
MELANIE WARRICK:
It's thermonuclear.

00:35:25.330 --> 00:35:27.747
[LAUGHTER]

00:35:27.747 --> 00:35:29.330
You could have
multiple ones reacting.

00:35:29.330 --> 00:35:30.920
MARK MANDEL: You can have
multiple functions reacting

00:35:30.920 --> 00:35:33.120
to a single Pub/Sub
topic push, seeing

00:35:33.120 --> 00:35:34.470
their multiple subscribers.

00:35:34.470 --> 00:35:35.780
So that's a really
nice extensible way

00:35:35.780 --> 00:35:38.270
to kind of working around the
limitations we have right now.

00:35:38.270 --> 00:35:39.936
But we'll have a link
in the show notes,

00:35:39.936 --> 00:35:41.780
and that's available in GitHub.

